[MAGNUM_OPUS_ENGINE]: TASK CONJURATION RESPONSE: {:__divine_interrupt=>:step_completed, :result=>"# Validatio — Security & Performance Audit\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL FINDINGS\n\n### C1: MSP Buffer Over-Read in MSP_SET_PID_ADVANCED\n**File:** `<file>src/main/msp/msp.c</file>` line 2169\n**Severity:** High — buffer over-read on malformed MSP packets\n\nThe `sbufBytesRemaining(src) >= 7` guard at line 2169 checks for 7 bytes, but the block reads **9 bytes**:\n- D_MIN: 5 reads (real, USE_D_MIN is defined)\n- INTEGRATED_YAW: 2 dummy reads (undef'd in OrniFlight section, but still consumed)\n- `flap_base_frequency`: 1 read (line 2191)\n- `flap_base_amplitude`: 1 read (line 2192)\n\nA client sending exactly 7 bytes after `antiGravityMode` causes `sbufReadU8()` to advance `src->ptr` 2 bytes past `src->end`. The streambuf implementation has **zero bounds checking** — `sbufReadU8` is `return *src->ptr++` directly. This reads adjacent stack/heap memory.\n\n**Root cause:** The OrniFlight `flap_base_frequency`/`flap_base_amplitude` extension was added inside the existing MSP API 1.41 block without expanding the guard from `>= 7` to `>= 9`.\n\n**Fix:** Change guard to `sbufBytesRemaining(src) >= 9`.\n\n### C2: Division by Zero — flap_base_frequency=0\n**File:** `<file>src/main/flight/pid.c</file>` line 698\n**Severity:** High — NaN/Infinity propagation in wing ODE, potential flight controller lockup\n\n```c\ntcommand = (rc_throttle - 480.0)\n    * ((1.0 / (0.1 * (float)servoConfig()->flap_base_frequency))  // ← DIV/0\n        + ...);\n```\n\nWhen `flap_base_frequency == 0`: `1.0/(0.1*0.0)` = `1.0/0.0` = `+Inf`. This propagates through `omegadot` → `omega` → `theta` → `sin(theta)` → `NaN`, collapsing the entire PID loop.\n\n**CLI range:** `{0, 255}` — zero is explicitly allowed\n**MSP bypass:** MSP_SET_PID_ADVANCED writes `sbufReadU8(src)` directly with no validation — can set any 0-255 value\n\n**Fix:** Change CLI min from 0 to 1 (`{1, 255}`), and add guard in `calculateFlappingFromThrottle`: `if (flap_base_frequency == 0) return;`\...
