            errorRate *= 1.0f - (1.0f - ondasAuthority) * (float)servo...\",\"range\":[1569,1640],\"execution_time\":0.021},\"args\":{\"range\":[1570,1640],\"path\":\"src/main/flight/pid.c\"},\"execution_time\":0.021},{\"content\":\"Now I have the complete picture. Let me also check one more thing — the `prevFlappingSinusoid` usage in SSFF:\",\"args\":null,\"result\":null},{\"id\":\"call_00_LspsEpdnp6WjqLtQVlSy1212\",\"name\":\"run_command\",\"result\":{\"ok\":true,\"exit_status\":0,\"result\":\"Command output: 88:static FAST_RAM_ZERO_INIT float flappingSinusoid;\\n89:static FAST_RAM_ZERO_INIT float flappingCosinusoid;\\n98:static FAST_RAM_ZERO_INIT float prevFlappingSinusoid;\\n602:    if (prevFlappingSinusoid * flappingSinusoid &lt;= 0.0f\\n603:        &amp;&amp; prevFlappingSinusoid != flappi...\",\"execution_time\":0.11},\"args\":{\"cmd\":\"grep -n \\\"prevFlapping\\\\|flappingSinusoid\\\\|flappingCosinusoid\\\" src/main/flight/pid.c\"},\"execution_time\":0.111}]", :created_at=>"2026-07-29 12:57:31"}
entry {:prompt=>"meditate further about half cycle duration which is more interesting and has more implications in stabilization. ondas was my first approach add extra methods but we may replace and alter it freely. now i think were focusing on the potential of introducing ferocity locally or even taking it away. in the other code we set base up or downstroke ferocity via rc channel as in the code, but i think in our code a more automatic, guided approach is more interesting. when stabilization is off in our code, the base ferocity values should be integrated not as extra rc channels anymore but already mixed into elevator for example according to model settings, but lets think about how to integrate into stabilization", :answer=>"---\n\n## Meditation on Half-Cycle Duration as Control Surface\n\nThe trapezoidal formulation reveals something the tanh formulation obscured: **ferocity is not one parameter — it is two independent control dimensions** that were collapsed into one.\n\n### The Two Dimensions of Ferocity\n\n```\nDimension 1 — DWELL (amplitude of \"snap\"):\n  d = f/8  →  fraction of half-cycle spent motionless at extreme\n  At f=0: wing traces full cosine, always moving\n  At f=8: wing snaps instantaneously then waits 50% of half-cycle\n\nDimension 2 — DURATION (temporal span of each half):\n  T_half ∝ 1/(8−f)  →  softer = longer half-cycle = slower flap frequency\n  Asymmetric fd ≠ fu: one half takes longer than the other\n```\n\nIn the Gralha Azul RC-channel model, these were fused — CH7/CH8 set one value that controlled both dwell *and* duration for each stroke direction. But they are conceptually separable, and separating them opens the stabilization architecture.\n\n### Half-Cycle Duration as Control Moment Generator\n\nWhen `fd ≠ fu`, the downstroke and upstroke occupy different fractions of the cycle. The wing spends more time producing thrust in one direction, creating a **net torque**.\n\n```\npitchMoment ∝ (T_down − T_up) / (T_down + T_up)\n            ∝ ((8−fd)⁻¹ − (8−fu)⁻¹) / ((8−fd)⁻¹ + (8−fu)⁻¹)\n```\n\nThis is slow control — you can't change ferocity mid-stroke; it takes effect on the *next* half-cycle. But it is physically efficient: rather than fighting the wing's natural motion with servo offsets (which waste energy), you **change what \"natural\" means**.\n\n### Three-Tier Stabilization Architecture\n\nThe insight is that different PID terms operate at different timescales and should use different mechanisms:\n\n| Timescale | Mechanism | PID Term | Physical Effect |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|-----------------|\n| **Stroke-level** (~80 ms) | Ferocity asymmetry `fd − fu` | **I-term** | Net thrust bias — \"the ornithopter always pitches down due to CG\" |\n| **Intra-stroke** (~8 ms) | Authority-gated servo mixing | **P + D terms** | Instantaneous correction — gust response, angular rate damping |\n| **Pre-PID** | Feed-forward subtraction | *(none)* | Remove known flap artifact from gyro signal |\n\nThis replaces the old scheme where `ondas_gain2` tried to modulate the I-term input at 12 Hz (which was broken — it made the integrator chase the flap frequency). Now the I-term has a *physically correct* output: it changes the wing motion itself.\n\n### What Changes in Practice\n\n**Before (old ONDAS):**\n```\nI-term accumulates → multiplied by ondas_gain2 oscillation → added to servo offset\n```\nThe I-term tried to correct a persistent bias by wiggling the servo at 12 Hz. Wrong mechanism.\n\n**After (proposed):**\n```\nI-term accumulates → mapped to Δferocity (fd−fu bias) → changes flapping waveform\nP+D term → passes through authority gate → added to servo offset  \n```\nThe I-term corrects persistent bias by making the wing flap asymmetrically. The P+D term handles transients through instantaneous servo mixing. Each term uses the mechanism that matches its timescale.\n\n### Ferocity Baseline Without Extra Channels\n\nWhen stabilization is off (passthrough/angle mode), the elevator stick already encodes a pitch command. Instead of requiring separate CH7/CH8 channels for ferocity, the **elevator channel maps directly to Δferocity**:\n\n```\nfd = baseFerocity + elevatorBias * mixGain\nfu = baseFerocity − elevatorBias * mixGain\n```\n\nWhere `baseFerocity` comes from throttle or a CLI setting, and `elevatorBias` is the elevator channel deflection. The `mixGain` is a new tuning parameter: how much pitch command translates to ferocity asymmetry vs. servo mixing.\n\nWhen stabilization is on, the I-term output replaces (or adds to) the elevator bias. The I-term *is* the automatic ferocity asymmetry.\n\n### The ONDAS Gate Remains — But Narrower\n\nThe authority gate still serves a critical function for P and D: even with asymmetric ferocity, the wing still has zero velocity at extremes. No mechanism can produce a control moment when the wing isn't moving. The gate simply prevents the PID from trying.\n\nBut with ferocity asymmetry handling the steady-state bias, the remaining corrections are smaller and more transient — the gate can be tighter without losing control authority.\n\n---\n\nShall I sketch this three-tier architecture in code?", :tool_call_count=>0, :execution_time=>57.60795, :timestamp=>nil, :tool_calls_json=>"[]", :created_at=>"2026-07-29 13:14:19"}
entry {:prompt=>"yes most your observations are correct. i wouldnt say you cannot change ferocity mid stroke... it can be i think , for example imagine a stroke was starting... raising ferocity could make it happen sooner, whil keeping rhythm and reducing waiting by making the rest of the phase delayed... also when for example a phase was delayed it could be delay then less or more... too big changes can be chaotic of course here and obviously we cannot change a stroke that occurred. and also our stabilization should keep the same frequency... it makes only try to alter the rhythm locally, but for sustained successful stabilization our effort must be to use exactly the beat to our advantage", :answer=>"You're right — I was too conservative. Ferocity isn't a half-cycle boundary parameter; it's a continuous control surface. Let me think deeper.\n\n---\n\n## Ferocity as a Continuous Modulation Surface\n\nThe key insight I missed: in the trapezoidal formulation, ferocity doesn't just set the stroke shape at the start — it defines a **mapping from phase to position** that can be warped at any point. Changing ferocity mid-stroke doesn't break anything; it just changes the remaining trajectory of the current half-cycle.\n\n### What Mid-Stroke Ferocity Change Actually Does\n\nConsider a downstroke in progress, phase θ ∈ [0, π), current ferocity f=4 (dwell=25%):\n\n```\nPhase progress: 0 ────[dwell]────→ dh ────[ramp]────→ π−dh ────[dwell]────→ π\n                                         ↑ we are here (just entered movement)\n```\n\nNow PID demands more pitch-up thrust. We increase downstroke ferocity f=4→6:\n\n```\nThe remaining ramp compresses: what was going to take the full movement window\nnow completes faster. The wing reaches the bottom dwell sooner. \n```\n\nBut the wing then waits longer in the bottom dwell (dh goes from 25%→37.5%) before the upstroke. **Total half-cycle duration doesn't change** — the time saved in ramp is exactly spent in dwell. The frequency is preserved automatically by the trapezoidal geometry.\n\nThis is the physical equivalent of \"snap harder, then rest longer\" — which is exactly what birds do when they need asymmetric thrust without changing their wingbeat frequency.\n\n### The Architecture This Implies\n\nInstead of the old two-layer scheme:\n\n```\nOld:  PID → servo offset + authority gate → fights wing motion\n```\n\nWe get a three-layer scheme where each PID term modulates a different aspect of the wing trajectory:\n\n```\nLayer 1 — I-TERM → FEROCITY ASYMMETRY (half-cycle bias)\n  iTermAccumulator → Δf = fd − fu\n  Persistent bias → asymmetric stroke ratio → net torque\n  Slow, cumulative, aerodynamic\n\nLayer 2 — P+D → FEROCITY MODULATION (intra-stroke warping)  \n  pTerm + dTerm → δf(θ) applied to current half-cycle\n  Transient correction → trajectory warping → immediate torque adjustment\n  Fast, within-stroke, automatically frequency-preserving\n\nLayer 3 — FEED-FORWARD → GYRO CLEANING (pre-PID)\n  Subtract predicted flap artifact from gyro before PID sees it\n  Removes the known 12 Hz oscillation from the error signal\n```\n\n### The ONDAS Gate Becomes Unnecessary\n\nThis is the deepest implication. We don't need to gate PID output by wing velocity because **the PID output doesn't directly drive the servo anymore**. Instead, it modulates the waveform. The wing's own physics provides the \"gating\":\n\n- At midstroke (high velocity): ferocity change → immediate trajectory change → immediate torque. **Natural high authority.**\n- At extreme (dwell): ferocity change → dwell duration change → torque delayed to next ramp. **Natural low authority, but not zero — the correction is queued, not discarded.**\n\nThe gate was a workaround for the wrong control architecture. By routing PID through the wing's own motion generation, authority becomes inherent rather than imposed.\n\n### The Elevator → ΔFerocity Mapping (Stab Off)\n\nWhen stabilization is off, the pilot's elevator stick maps naturally:\n\n```\nΔf = elevatorStick * elevatorToFerocityGain\n\n// Split around base:\nfd = baseFerocity + Δf/2    // clamped [0, 8]\nfu = baseFerocity − Δf/2    // clamped [0, 8]\n```\n\nThe `elevatorToFerocityGain` becomes a tuning parameter: how much pitch control comes from stroke asymmetry vs. traditional servo mixing.\n\nWhen stabilization is active:\n- **I-term** replaces (or adds to) the elevator's role in setting Δf\n- **P and D** modulate f(θ) within the stroke\n\n### Frequency Preservation Guarantee\n\nThe trapezoidal formulation has a self-balancing property: for any ferocity f ∈ [0,8], the half-cycle duration T = k/(8−f). If we increase f mid-ramp, the remaining phase advances faster, the wing reaches the bottom dwell sooner, and spends longer there. The total half-cycle time is invariant because:\n\n```\nT_total = T_dwell + T_ramp\n        = (d/2)·T_half + (1−d)·T_half  \n        = T_half · (1 − d/2)\n```\n\nWith d = f/8 and T_half ∝ 1/(8−f):\n\n```\nT_total ∝ (1 − f/16) / (8−f)\n```\n\nThis is NOT actually constant — it varies with f. At f=0: T_total ∝ 1/8. At f=8: T_total ∝ 0.5/0 → ∞ (degenerate, square wave has undefined timing in this model).\n\nSo the frequency isn't automatically preserved. We'd need explicit compensation. But that compensation is simple: **track phase progress explicitly, not time**.\n\n### Phase-Budget Tracking\n\nInstead of computing servo position from `sin(ωt)`, track a **phase accumulator** that advances at a ferocity-dependent rate:\n\n```\nphaseAdvancePerLoop = flapFrequency * dt * (8 − effectiveFerocity) / (8 − baseFerocity)\n```\n\nWhen `effectiveFerocity > baseFerocity`: phase advances faster now, will advance slower later (when ferocity drops below base to compensate). The modulation is a zero-sum redistribution of the phase budget within a cycle.\n\nThis is the correct primitive. It naturally preserves frequency without requiring explicit prediction, because any acceleration now creates a deceleration debt that the feedback loop will repay.\n\n---\n\nShall I implement this phase-budget + trapezoidal + three-tier architecture? The changes would touch `calculateFlappingFromThrottle` (new waveform), the PID application block (route P/D/I through ferocity modulation), and add the phase accumulator.", :tool_call_count=>0, :execution_time=>86.30057, :timestamp=>nil, :tool_calls_json=>"[]", :created_at=>"2026-07-29 13:24:44"}
entry {:prompt=>"upstroke and downstroke create thrust (also differential thrust), but downstroke creates more pitch up moment and upstroke creates some pitch down moment, however an upstroke with a lower centre of flapping would still create pitch up moment, however less than downstroke with lower centre of flapping of course. my ondas system tried to connect to this but only loosely yet, the exact set point and angular speed and waiting duration are important... however we dont really wait which would be gliding... instead now we make delayed strokes in the opposite direction... that is a careful energy saving approach... stabilize rhythm by relax, by a breathing pause... yous get what i mean?", :answer=>"Yes, I hear you now. Let me think about this differently.\n\n---\n\n## The Breathing Pause\n\nYou're describing something more subtle than \"dwell\" — not an absence of motion, not gliding, but a **relaxation that is itself part of the rhythm**. A pause that breathes. The wing doesn't stop; it **commits to staying at the extreme**, which is aerodynamically different from gliding. In gliding the wing is still producing lift; in the breathing pause the wing is at the extreme position, the servo holding against spring force, the airflow stalled or nearly so — minimal energy expenditure, yet rhythmically integral.\n\nThe pause is the **control surface with the best energy economy**: you can lengthen or shorten it almost for free, because holding position costs less power than moving through air.\n\n### Three Parameters, Three Physical Effects\n\n| Parameter | What it controls | Energy cost | Speed of response |\n|-----------|-----------------|-------------|-------------------|\n| **Set point** (center of arc) | Thrust vector angle | High — shifts entire stroke against air | Instant (servo offset) |\n| **Angular speed** (ramp steepness) | Thrust magnitude | High — moves wing faster through air | Fast (within current ramp) |\n| **Pause duration** (breathing) | Timing of next thrust pulse | Low — holding position | Slow (affects next half-cycle) |\n\nThe breathing pause is the most elegant tool for stabilization because it costs almost nothing: you're not asking the servo to fight harder, you're asking it to **wait longer before the next effort**. The correction is redistributing time, not adding force.\n\n### The Beat Itself as Stabilization\n\nYour insight about \"using exactly the beat to our advantage\" means: the PID shouldn't fight the flap rhythm by injecting counter-movements. It should **slightly deform the rhythm** so that the wing's own thrust pulses arrive at moments that correct the error.\n\nConcretely: if the ornithopter is pitching up too fast, you don't fight the upstroke. You let the upstroke happen, but **shorten the bottom pause** so the next downstroke (which creates pitch-up moment) arrives sooner, and **lengthen the top pause** so the next upstroke (which creates pitch-down moment) is delayed. The rhythm itself becomes the control output.\n\nThe PID terms map naturally:\n- **P-term**: immediate angular speed modulation within current ramp — \"push harder now\"\n- **D-term**: pause duration modulation — \"delay the next opposite stroke\"  \n- **I-term**: set point bias — \"shift the center of flapping permanently\"\n\n### What This Means for Code\n\nThe phase accumulator I sketched earlier is correct but incomplete. It needs to track not just phase position but **which pause we're in** and route PID terms to the appropriate parameter:\n\n```\nAt midstroke (ramp):        P-term → angularSpeed modulation\nAt extreme (pause):          D-term → pauseDuration modulation  \nAlways:                      I-term → setPoint bias accumulation\n```\n\nThe breathing pause is the D-term's natural output channel. D-term measures *rate of error change* — it predicts where the error is heading. Using it to modulate pause duration means: \"I see where this is going, I'll adjust when the next corrective thrust arrives.\"\n\nThis is fundamentally different from the old ONDAS gate approach, which tried to silence the PID when the wing wasn't moving. Instead, we route each term to the control surface that's active at that moment in the cycle. Nothing is gated — everything has a place to go.\n\n---\n\nShall I implement this three-channel routing into the existing `calculateFlappingFromThrottle` and PID application?", :tool_call_count=>0, :execution_time=>32.337161, :timestamp=>nil, :tool_calls_json=>"[]", :created_at=>"2026-07-29 13:48:58"}
entry {:prompt=>"yes do it", :answer=>"The three-channel breathing-pause architecture is now implemented across all files. Here's what changed:\n\n---\n\n## Architecture: PID → Wing Trajectory Modulation\n\n### The Three Channels\n\n| Channel | PID Term | Modulates | Code Path | Gain Param |\n|---------|----------|-----------|-----------|------------|\n| **1** | **P** | k₀ (phase drive) | `k0 *= flappingPhaseModulation` | `ondas_gain` (20) |\n| **2** | **D** | ferocity (wave sharpness) | `fDown/fUp *= (1 + dMod)` | `ondas_gain2` (20) |\n| **3** | **I** | up/down asymmetry | `fUp += iBias; fDown -= iBias` | `ondas_gain3` (10) |\n\n### How It Works\n\n```\ngyro → errorRate → PID → pidData[PITCH].P/.I/.D\n                              │          │          │\n                              ▼          ▼          ▼\n                         k0 scaling  f-modulation  f-asymmetry\n                              │          │          │\n                              └──────────┼──────────┘\n                                         ▼\n                              calculateFlappingFromThrottle\n                              (on next iteration, ~0.25ms lag)\n                                         │\n                                         ▼\n                              wing ODE → shapedWave → servos\n```\n\n**P-term (\"push harder now\")**: When the craft needs correction, `k0` increases → wing accelerates through the current ramp → corrective thrust arrives sooner. When error is small, `k0` relaxes back. Modulation is symmetric (always ∈ [0.5, 2.0]) — it doesn't care which half-cycle we're in.\n\n**D-term (\"delay the next opposite stroke\")**: When error is accelerating (situation worsening), ferocity increases → wave sharpens → more time in the breathing pause → the next opposite stroke is delayed. When error is decelerating, ferocity relaxes → wave softens → less pause → next stroke arrives sooner. D-term predicts and adjusts the *rhythm*.\n\n**I-term (\"shift the center of flapping\")**: Persistent error biases upstroke vs downstroke ferocity. If nose keeps pitching up, I-term grows positive → fUp increases, fDown decreases → more pitch-down thrust per upstroke, less pitch-up per downstroke. This is the sustained trim channel.\n\n### What Was Removed\n\n- **ONDAS authority gate**: No longer multiplies `errorRate` by wing velocity. The wing's own physics provides authority — at midstroke, P-term modulation of k0 has immediate effect; at extreme, D-term adjusts the pause.\n- **Feed-forward subtraction**: No longer subtracts `flappingDerivative` from errorRate. The flap artifact in the gyro now passes through to PID, which is fine because PID output modulates waveform parameters rather than fighting the wing directly.\n- **D-term gyro delta gate**: No longer attenuates D by `ondasAuthority`. D-term is now useful at all phases — it controls breathing pause depth.\n\n### Files Modified\n\n- <file>src/main/flight/pid.c</file> — 3 new globals, modified `applyFerocityWaveShaping` (2 new params), modified `calculateFlappingFromThrottle` (k0 modulation, pass params), replaced ONDAS gate in PID loop with three-channel computation, removed D-term gate\n- <file>src/main/flight/servos.h</file> — updated field comments\n- <file>src/main/flight/servos.c</file> — new defaults (20/20/10)\n- <file>src/main/cli/settings.c</file> — updated CLI descriptions", :tool_call_count=>85, :execution_time=>456.44744, :timestamp=>nil, :tool_calls_json=>"[{\"content\":\"\",\"args\":null,\"result\":null},{\"id\":\"call_00_wxYhKXKws3HPqTjSIpdh3612\",\"name\":\"file_overview\",\"result\":{\"notes_count\":3,\"notes_preview\":null,\"file_info\":{\"lines\":1794,\"size\":70085,\"last_modified\":\"2026-07-14 18:24:23 +0200\",\"path\":\"src/main/flight/pid.c\"},\"structural_summary\":{\"language\":\"ruby\",\"containers\":0,\"members\":0,\"imports\":0,\"exports\":0,\"total_symbols\":122},\"symbolic_overview\":\"246: loc targetPidLooptime\\n247: loc dT\\n248: loc pidFrequency\\n255: loc itermAccelerator\\n330: loc dtermNotchApplyFn\\n331: loc dtermLowpassApplyFn\\n332: loc ptermYawLowpassApplyFn\\n343: loc dTermNotchHz\\n345: loc dTermNotchHz\\n350: loc dtermNotchApplyFn\\n356: loc dtermNotchApplyFn\\n371: loc dtermLowpassApp...\",\"tag_cloud\":\"code: 6\\nferocity: 4\\nondas: 3\\narchitecture: 2\\noverview: 1\\nsimulation: 1\\nservo: 1\\nmixer: 1\\nornithopter: 1\\nformula: 1\\ngralha-azul: 1\\npid: 1\\nunified: 1\\nstructure: 1\",\"file_cloud\":\"src/main/flight/pid.c: 3\\nsrc/main/flight/servos.h: 3\\nsrc/main/cli/settings.c: 3\\nsrc/main/flight/servos.c: 2\\n~~/Users/d/Desktop/HOI_KOSMOI/paraeksperiment/Arduino/libraries/o-grande-codigo-da-gralha-azul/src/GralhaAzul_Padraos.h~~ (path not found): 1\\nsrc/main/flight/: 1\\nREADME.md: 1\\nONDAS.md: 1\\nsi...\",\"hermetic_overview\":[\"src/main/flight/pid.c>code*3,ferocity*3,ondas*3,architecture*2,pid*1\",\"src/main/cli/settings.c>architecture*2,code*2,ferocity*2,ondas*2,pid*1\",\"src/main/flight/servos.h>architecture*2,code*2,ferocity*2,ondas*2,pid*1\",\"ONDAS.md>architecture*1,code*1,ferocity*1,ondas*1\",\"sim_ferocity.rb>code*1,ferocity*1,ondas*1,simulation*1\",\"src/main/flight/servos.c>architecture*1,code*1,ferocity*1,ondas*1\"],\"execution_time\":0.058},\"args\":{\"path\":\"src/main/flight/pid.c\"},\"execution_time\":0.058},{\"id\":\"call_01_FW5NuAyiejXt1GK8nFHw5622\",\"name\":\"file_overview\",\"result\":{\"notes_count\":3,\"notes_preview\":null,\"file_info\":{\"lines\":174,\"size\":5982,\"last_modified\":\"2026-07-14 08:10:19 +0200\",\"path\":\"src/main/flight/servos.h\"},\"structural_summary\":{\"language\":\"ruby\",\"containers\":0,\"members\":25,\"imports\":0,\"exports\":0,\"total_symbols\":25},\"symbolic_overview\":\"29: con INPUT_STABILIZED_ROLL\\n52: con SERVO_GIMBAL_PITCH\\n53: con SERVO_GIMBAL_ROLL\\n54: con SERVO_FLAPS\\n55: con SERVO_FLAPPERON_1\\n56: con SERVO_FLAPPERON_2\\n57: con SERVO_RUDDER\\n58: con SERVO_ELEVATOR\\n59: con SERVO_THROTTLE\\n61: con SERVO_BICO